[{:db/id 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a7b8c64-0e50-5904-90c5-c8b0f74761de"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198156 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\nII. $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\nIII. $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198157 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\nII. $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\nIII. $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\: : \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198158 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\nII. $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\nIII. $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287741 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|4n|\\ge 8}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|4q|\\ge 8}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|4x|\\ge 8}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a7b8c64-0e50-5904-90c5-c8b0f74761de" :question/id #uuid "7a7b8c64-0e50-5904-90c5-c8b0f74761de" :question/math-academy-id "q-181771" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0}$\n\nII. $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0}$\n\nIII. $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57d58b579557ba0ea8c6179bd37209c8199eee83e17d79c74828a051aa1382d6.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=[-3,4].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=8,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0012b7e2-7b21-52ce-a8e6-93abfde08a7a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0012b7e2-7b21-52ce-a8e6-93abfde08a7a" :answer-field/id #uuid "0012b7e2-7b21-52ce-a8e6-93abfde08a7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a84f9d9-81b1-54ae-8ae5-8c05ca06f33d" "answer-f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" "answer-7a2e9409-26c5-5b50-9445-3a7e0cc1dff6" "answer-9a0ac365-22bb-5a1d-8ee4-097642b3a96a" "answer-dd52b8a6-5559-526c-9e66-cb99de5e9630"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a84f9d9-81b1-54ae-8ae5-8c05ca06f33d" :answer/id #uuid "4a84f9d9-81b1-54ae-8ae5-8c05ca06f33d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/id #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a2e9409-26c5-5b50-9445-3a7e0cc1dff6" :answer/id #uuid "7a2e9409-26c5-5b50-9445-3a7e0cc1dff6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a0ac365-22bb-5a1d-8ee4-097642b3a96a" :answer/id #uuid "9a0ac365-22bb-5a1d-8ee4-097642b3a96a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd52b8a6-5559-526c-9e66-cb99de5e9630" :answer/id #uuid "dd52b8a6-5559-526c-9e66-cb99de5e9630" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287740 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|5n|\\le 4}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|5q|\\le 4}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|5x|\\le 4}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198784 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$\n\nII. $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$\n\nIII. $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198797 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|2n|>5}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|2q|>5}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|2x|>5}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287715 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡n=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡q=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198792 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡n=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡q=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186420329 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi )=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2q\\pi )=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287717 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186420331 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287683 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(2{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186420330 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{1-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4{e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287713 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186420332 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287711 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x-1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x/2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
